  "response": "Adjudication in this field begins with the attempt to make growth itself measurable, since a doctrinal explanation and a sociological one predict different trajectories. Rodney Stark's The Rise of Christianity contains a quantitative projection of Christian population growth, as the aggregate figure it reaches for the end of the third century makes plain. A total of roughly 6 million Christians by the year 300 CE resulted from Stark's estimate, the estimate being that early Christianity grew at roughly 40 percent per decade. Stark calibrated his assumed growth rate against the documented expansion of the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints, and it is that modern comparison which supports setting the decadal rate where he set it.\n\nThe mechanism standing behind such arithmetic is social rather than theological. Stark holds that conversion follows pre-existing interpersonal attachments; in other words, on his account people adopt a new faith when their ties to members outweigh their ties to nonmembers. Prosopographic analysis of the individuals named in Paul's letters is used to test the social-network hypothesis, a procedure now standard in the discipline. Ramsay MacMullen argued that interpersonal networks rather than displays of miraculous power drove mass conversions in the Roman world. Evidence of that name-by-name kind is what indicates the soundness of Stark's argument that early converts were drawn disproportionately from the urban middle and upper strata of Greco-Roman cities. Charles Glock's deprivation theory, by contrast, holds that religious movements recruit disproportionately from relatively deprived populations.\n\nA second line of adjudication is documentary rather than inferential, and it turns on sources that were never produced to answer the historian's question. Roger Bagnall used personal names recorded in Egyptian papyri to estimate the proportion of Christians in the population, a labour spread over many thousands of documents. His onomastic data refute exponential models of Christian population growth. Scholars weigh such independent series precisely because a model's internal consistency cannot establish its demographic truth; the test is whether the names, inscriptions and papyri of a given province fall where the curve requires them to fall, province by province and generation by generation.\n\nWhat remains to be assessed is the content of the message alongside the practice of the communities that carried it. At least one of the following holds of the earliest congregations, and perhaps both of them do: early Christian doctrine promised adherents a bodily resurrection, and early Christian congregations supplied material relief to widows and the sick. The latter, the organized care of the vulnerable, is the kind of fact that confirms Glock's account of recruitment among the relatively deprived, since a movement whose attraction lay in such provision would draw its membership from those most in need of it. Disputes of this kind are therefore settled where the two bodies of evidence meet: a projected curve must survive contact with the documentary record, and a proposed motive for conversion must be matched by the recruitment pattern that the surviving sources actually display.",
